P2P File Sharing with Spring Boot
=================================

This application exposes the P2P file-sharing system through a Spring Boot REST API. The embedded tracker speaks the existing UDP protocol on port 5000, and seeders continue to serve chunks over TCP using the existing wire protocol.

Requirements
------------

- Java 17 or newer
- Maven 3.6 or newer
- Node.js 22 or newer and npm

Run the application from this directory:

	mvn spring-boot:run

The REST API listens on `http://127.0.0.1:8080`. Files are read from and written to `./data` by default. Override the storage directory with `--p2p.storage-dir=/path/to/data`.

React interface
---------------

Start the Spring Boot API first, then run the React development server in a second terminal:

	cd frontend
	npm install
	npm run dev

Open `http://127.0.0.1:5173`. The development server proxies API requests to Spring Boot on port 8080. The interface can upload files into the configured storage directory, seed them, search for peers by SHA-256, and track download progress.

To include the UI in the Spring Boot application jar:

	cd frontend
	npm install
	npm run build
	cd ..
	mvn package

The Vite build writes static assets to `src/main/resources/static`; Spring Boot then serves the interface from `http://127.0.0.1:8080`.

API
---

Check application status:

	curl http://127.0.0.1:8080/api/status

Place a file inside the storage directory, then calculate its SHA-256 hash:

	curl "http://127.0.0.1:8080/api/files/hash?fileName=sample.iso"

The React interface uploads files using `POST /api/files` as multipart form data and receives the file name, size, and SHA-256 hash.

Use the trash action in the shared library to stop seeding and delete a file from the configured storage directory. Completed and failed transfer entries can be cleared from the activity history; active downloads remain visible.

Start sharing a file. The peer TCP port must be reachable by other peers:

	curl -X POST http://127.0.0.1:8080/api/seeders \
	  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
	  -d '{"fileName":"sample.iso","peerIp":"127.0.0.1","peerPort":6000,"trackerHost":"127.0.0.1","trackerPort":5000}'

Find active seeders for a hash:

	curl "http://127.0.0.1:8080/api/peers?hash=SHA256_HASH"

List files this node is currently seeding:

	curl http://127.0.0.1:8080/api/seeders

Stop seeding and delete a file from local storage:

	curl -X DELETE "http://127.0.0.1:8080/api/files?fileName=sample.iso"

Start a download. The response is HTTP 202 and contains a job ID; the completed file is stored under the configured storage directory. The app verifies the SHA-256 hash and then attempts to seed the downloaded file on the supplied peer port.

	curl -X POST http://127.0.0.1:8080/api/downloads \
	  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
	  -d '{"targetFile":"copy.iso","fileHash":"SHA256_HASH","trackerHost":"127.0.0.1","trackerPort":5000,"peerIp":"127.0.0.1","peerPort":7000}'

Poll the returned ID for status and progress:

	curl http://127.0.0.1:8080/api/downloads/JOB_ID

Configuration
-------------

Important properties can be set in `src/main/resources/application.properties` or passed as command-line arguments:

- `server.port` (default `8080`): REST API port.
- `p2p.storage-dir` (default `./data`): allowed file storage root.
- `p2p.tracker.enabled` (default `true`): run the embedded UDP tracker. Set to `false` on peer nodes that use a separate tracker.
- `p2p.tracker.udp-port` (default `5000`): embedded tracker UDP port.
- `p2p.chunk-size` (default `524288`): chunk size; must match other peers.

Use one embedded tracker per tracker host. On peer hosts that should not run a tracker, start with `--p2p.tracker.enabled=false` and point requests to the tracker host. Make sure the REST API port and peer TCP ports are reachable as appropriate for your network.

Build and test
--------------

	mvn test
	mvn package
